WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.037
Z-scores can also help us identify observations that are far from the center of a distribution.

00:00:06.037 --> 00:00:12.227
In this example, most values are around fifty, but one value is one hundred twenty.

00:00:12.227 --> 00:00:20.627
We calculate the z-score for every observation and then create a Boolean mask where the absolute z-score is

00:00:20.627 --> 00:00:25.412
greater than two. Click Run and inspect both the z-scores and the flagged values.

00:00:25.412 --> 00:00:31.882
The important lesson is not that two standard deviations is a universal outlier rule.

00:00:31.882 --> 00:00:37.422
It is not. The useful pattern is: calculate a diagnostic measure, create a condition,

00:00:37.422 --> 00:00:40.417
and flag observations for investigation.

00:00:40.417 --> 00:00:44.877
Do not automatically delete a value simply because it looks unusual.

00:00:44.877 --> 00:00:51.437
An unusual value may be a data-entry error, but it may also be a rare and important real

00:00:51.437 --> 00:00:57.796
event. The analyst's job is to flag first, investigate second, and decide based on context.
